{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No statewide rent control; municipal rent control ordinances allowed","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law establishing, governing or controlling rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, which locally created boards enforce.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rentals in New Jersey; rent control applies only where a municipality has adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings; exemption runs 30 years from completion"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control ordinances for 30 years; government-subsidized rents may also be exempt.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increases: no mid-lease increase and unconscionability standard","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent during a lease term; the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered at the increased rent, and any increase must not be unconscionable and must follow municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants statewide; increases must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance where one exists.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No rent increase to cover condominium or cooperative conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, regardless of eligibility for senior or disabled protected tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"HUD- and NJHMFA-financed housing exempt from municipal rent control","requirement":"Housing developments owned or subsidized by HUD, HUD-insured developments with economic problems, and projects financed by NJHMFA are not subject to municipal rent control ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"HUD-owned, HUD-subsidized or HUD-insured developments and NJHMFA-financed projects, which are not subject to municipal rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"housing financed or subsidized by HUD or NJHMFA"},"exemptions":"Such developments are exempt from municipal rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"rents fixed and controlled by the New Jersey Housing and Mortgage Finance Agency (NJHMFA) in projects it finances are not subject to municipal rent control ordinances.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.65,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Good cause required to evict or refuse to renew a residential lease","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential unit only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and only for good cause, which the landlord must prove in court.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence; the Anti-Eviction Act applies statewide.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential dwelling units used as a residence"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Notice to Quit required for good cause evictions","requirement":"For every good cause eviction except nonpayment of rent, the landlord must describe the cause in detail in a written Notice to Quit; a Notice to Cease may also be required.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All good cause evictions of residential tenants; nonpayment of rent is excepted from the Notice to Quit requirement.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential good cause evictions"},"exemptions":"Evictions for nonpayment of rent do not require a Notice to Quit.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A “Notice to Quit” is required for all good cause evictions, except for an eviction for nonpayment of rent","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New owner's duty to obtain and return security deposit on sale","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices, paying interest and returning it.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Security deposits for residential rental property that is sold or transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for withholding deposit of a tenant receiving public assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds the security deposit of a tenant receiving State or federal financial assistance, including rental assistance, may be liable for a civil penalty.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who have received State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"tenants receiving State or federal financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceeding under the Penalty Enforcement Law; recovered amounts go to the State entity that made the deposits.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Criminal penalty for unlawful use of security deposit monies","requirement":"A person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly person and fined at least $200 or imprisoned up to 30 days, or both.","key_value":"fine of not less than $200, or up to 30 days' imprisonment","coverage_conditions":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-25","quoted_span":"may be subject to a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord may charge tenant for credit report and reasonable application fees","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant the cost of a credit or tenant screening report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants of residential rental units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"prospective residential tenants"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.6,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"LAD protection against housing discrimination based on source of lawful income","requirement":"Landlords and rental agencies may not discriminate in renting housing based on source of lawful income or other protected characteristics, and must give equal treatment in rental housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex residences as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all landlord-tenant relationships, subject to owner-occupied and single-sex exceptions"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned for and occupied by one sex, as to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
